        ":question/problem": "Find the general solution of the following differential equation using the method of integrating factors.\n\n$$\n\\dfrac {\\textrm{d}y} {\\textrm{d}x} + \\dfrac {3} {x} y = \\dfrac{\\cos x}{x^2}, \\qquad x \\gt 0.\n$$",
        ":question/worked-solution": "This equation is in the standard form\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x} + P(x)y = Q(x)\n$$\n\nwith $P(x) = \\dfrac{3}{x}$ and $Q(x) = \\dfrac{\\cos x}{x^2}.$ We proceed to find an integrating factor $I(x)$ using the definition:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I(x) & ={e}^{\\int P(x)\\,\\text{d}x} \\\\  & ={e}^{\\int (3/x)\\,\\text{d}x} \\\\  & ={e}^{3\\operatorname{ln}⁡x} \\\\  & ={e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{3})} \\\\  & ={x}^{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we multiply our differential equation through by $I(x) = x^3$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{3}\\cdot (\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+\\frac{3}{x}y) & ={x}^{3}\\cdot \\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{{x}^{2}} \\\\ {x}^{3}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+3{x}^{2}\\cdot y & =x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we rewrite the left hand side as $\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}}{\\textrm{d}x}\\left(y\\cdot I(x)\\right)$ and carry out the integration:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(y{x}^{3}) & =x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x \\\\ \\int \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(y{x}^{3})\\text{d}x & =\\int x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ y{x}^{3} & =\\int x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo solve this integral, we integrate by parts. \n\n$$\n\\int uv'\\,\\textrm d x = uv - \\int u' v\\,\\textrm d x\n$$\n\nLet $u = x, u' = 1, v' = \\cos x,$ and $v = \\sin x.$ Then, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y{x}^{3} & =\\int x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ y{x}^{3} & =x\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-\\int \\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ y{x}^{3} & =x\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+C \\\\ y & =\\frac{x\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{{x}^{3}}+\\frac{C}{{x}^{3}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"
      },
